    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "China is currently in the pilot stage of developing its long-term care insurance (LTCI). Given the urban-rural divide seen in various aspects of Chinese society, this paper argues that China needs to put more effort into the introduction of LTCI in rural areas. This issue has become more and more important in China, especially because of the low birthrate and rapidly aging society.\r\nFirst, based on demographic data, the combination of an unbalanced elderly population and unique issues, such as migration of adult children to urban areas, the worse health status of rural elderly people, and lower income for the rural population are the description of rural elderly people’s necessity around the LTCI issue. The urban population is and will be larger than the rural one, but the high aging rate and these unique issues in rural areas make the provision of aged care more challenging.\r\nSecond, the analysis of official documents at national and local levels demonstrates that neither the central government nor the local governments make sufficient efforts to introduce LTCI in rural areas. Although the central government is conscious of the gaps between urban and rural residents in aged care, from the national viewpoint, China’s LTCI is still mainly in the stage of trials in urban areas. At a local level, some pilot cities have expanded their LTCI into rural areas in their implementation of the Guiding Opinions. Nevertheless, their efforts to promote and optimize LTCI for rural residents do not seem sufficient because there is no special consideration for the structure of rural LTCI, despite the gaps in their living conditions.\r\nThird, the calculation of the opportunity costs of intergenerational informal care in rural areas makes it clear that the potential effects of more commitments to introducing LTCI in rural areas can bring about substantive benefits to Chinese society. \r\nFrom these perspectives, this paper asserts that China should dedicate greater attention to the implementation of LTCI in its rural areas.",
